1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,773
A candle under a glass goes out.

2
00:00:02,773 --> 00:00:05,720
A rocket carries its
own oxygen into space.

3
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:08,319
Every flame uses up oxygen.

4
00:00:08,319 --> 00:00:09,570
But how much?

5
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:12,612
Aarav watches the stove in his kitchen.

6
00:00:12,612 --> 00:00:16,372
His family cooks on natural
gas, which is mostly methane.

7
00:00:16,722 --> 00:00:19,755
How much oxygen does
each gram of it use up?

8
00:00:20,255 --> 00:00:23,520
By the end, you will read
any equation like a recipe.

9
00:00:24,170 --> 00:00:26,409
First, what is a chemical equation?

10
00:00:26,759 --> 00:00:29,180
It is a short way to write a reaction.

11
00:00:29,180 --> 00:00:32,311
Reactants, the starting
substances, go on the left.

12
00:00:32,711 --> 00:00:34,370
Products go on the right.

13
00:00:34,370 --> 00:00:35,835
The arrow means gives.

14
00:00:36,235 --> 00:00:37,899
Brackets tell the state.

15
00:00:37,899 --> 00:00:41,502
g gas, l liquid, s solid,
a q dissolved in water.

16
00:00:41,902 --> 00:00:44,556
Stoichiometry is the
maths of these amounts.

17
00:00:44,556 --> 00:00:47,996
From Greek: stoicheion,
element, and metron, measure.

18
00:00:48,646 --> 00:00:51,552
Let's burn methane on our molecule bench.

19
00:00:51,552 --> 00:00:53,893
One carbon holds four hydrogens.

20
00:00:54,243 --> 00:00:59,026
Oxygen gas is two oxygen atoms,
sharing two pairs of electrons.

21
00:00:59,426 --> 00:01:04,006
As written, methane plus oxygen
gives carbon dioxide plus water.

22
00:01:04,406 --> 00:01:05,306
Watch.

23
00:01:05,003 --> 00:01:08,828
The old bonds break, the atoms
move, and new bonds form.

24
00:01:09,228 --> 00:01:11,166
Carbon takes two oxygens.

25
00:01:11,166 --> 00:01:14,428
Hydrogen pairs up with
oxygen to make water.

26
00:01:15,078 --> 00:01:16,438
Check the counter.

27
00:01:16,438 --> 00:01:18,217
Hydrogen, four and two.

28
00:01:18,217 --> 00:01:19,946
Oxygen, two and three.

29
00:01:19,946 --> 00:01:20,989
Not balanced.

30
00:01:21,339 --> 00:01:23,583
Let's balance it, one atom at a time.

31
00:01:23,933 --> 00:01:24,846
Step one.

32
00:01:24,846 --> 00:01:26,725
Carbon is already equal.

33
00:01:26,725 --> 00:01:28,216
One on each side.

34
00:01:28,966 --> 00:01:29,866
Step two.

35
00:01:29,769 --> 00:01:32,787
Four hydrogens in methane,
two in each water.

36
00:01:32,787 --> 00:01:35,021
So put a two in front of water.

37
00:01:35,771 --> 00:01:36,687
Step three.

38
00:01:36,687 --> 00:01:39,923
Oxygen on the right is
now two plus two, four.

39
00:01:40,673 --> 00:01:42,970
So put a two in front of oxygen.

40
00:01:42,970 --> 00:01:44,084
Four and four.

41
00:01:44,834 --> 00:01:45,833
Count again.

42
00:01:45,833 --> 00:01:47,254
Every atom matches.

43
00:01:47,254 --> 00:01:48,154
Balanced.

44
00:01:48,687 --> 00:01:51,809
Now read the balanced
equation like a recipe.

45
00:01:52,159 --> 00:01:53,059
In moles.

46
00:01:53,056 --> 00:01:57,259
One methane, two oxygen, one
carbon dioxide, two water.

47
00:01:58,009 --> 00:01:59,319
In grams per mole.

48
00:01:59,319 --> 00:02:02,261
Sixteen, thirty two,
forty four and eighteen.

49
00:02:03,011 --> 00:02:08,203
So sixteen grams of methane burns sixty
four grams of oxygen, giving forty

50
00:02:08,203 --> 00:02:10,277
four and thirty six grams.

51
00:02:11,027 --> 00:02:13,373
Eighty grams in, eighty grams out.

52
00:02:13,373 --> 00:02:14,667
Mass is conserved.

53
00:02:15,417 --> 00:02:20,859
In litres, at STP, one mole of gas
fills twenty two point seven litres.

54
00:02:20,859 --> 00:02:22,634
Same one to two ratio.

55
00:02:23,284 --> 00:02:24,455
Side by side.

56
00:02:24,455 --> 00:02:26,299
In moles, one to two.

57
00:02:26,299 --> 00:02:28,803
In grams, sixteen to sixty four.

58
00:02:29,203 --> 00:02:30,482
Pause and think.

59
00:02:30,482 --> 00:02:33,695
One mole of methane needs
two moles of oxygen.

60
00:02:33,695 --> 00:02:37,396
So does one gram of methane
need two grams of oxygen?

61
00:02:40,596 --> 00:02:41,496
No!

62
00:02:41,154 --> 00:02:42,269
Four grams.

63
00:02:42,269 --> 00:02:46,427
One oxygen molecule weighs
twice one methane molecule.

64
00:02:46,427 --> 00:02:49,214
Equations count moles, not grams.

65
00:02:49,864 --> 00:02:52,166
Many reactions happen in solutions.

66
00:02:52,166 --> 00:02:54,928
There, we count moles with concentration.

67
00:02:55,328 --> 00:02:57,634
Your NCERT book gives four ways.

68
00:02:57,634 --> 00:03:01,203
Mass percent, mole fraction,
molarity and molality.

69
00:03:01,603 --> 00:03:05,926
The most used is molarity: moles
of solute per litre of solution.

70
00:03:06,676 --> 00:03:12,515
NCERT's example: four grams of sodium
hydroxide in two hundred fifty millilitres.

71
00:03:13,265 --> 00:03:16,583
At forty grams per mole,
that is zero point one mole.

72
00:03:17,333 --> 00:03:21,601
In zero point two five litres,
that gives zero point four molar.

73
00:03:22,251 --> 00:03:23,353
Remember this.

74
00:03:23,353 --> 00:03:26,691
A balanced equation is a
recipe written in moles.

75
00:03:27,391 --> 00:03:28,995
Now, Aarav's question.

76
00:03:29,295 --> 00:03:32,305
How much oxygen burns
four grams of methane?

77
00:03:32,305 --> 00:03:34,154
And how much water forms?

78
00:03:34,654 --> 00:03:39,122
Four grams over sixteen is zero
point two five moles of methane.

79
00:03:39,872 --> 00:03:43,052
Twice that, zero point
five moles of oxygen.

80
00:03:43,052 --> 00:03:44,721
That is sixteen grams.

81
00:03:45,471 --> 00:03:48,831
And zero point five moles
of water, nine grams.

82
00:03:49,581 --> 00:03:54,299
So four grams of methane uses up
four times its mass in oxygen.

83
00:03:54,949 --> 00:03:56,412
Now, the exam trap.

84
00:03:56,762 --> 00:04:01,481
Some students calculate from the
unbalanced equation, one methane to one

85
00:04:01,481 --> 00:04:02,381
oxygen.

86
00:04:02,587 --> 00:04:04,136
That halves the answer.

87
00:04:04,136 --> 00:04:06,754
Balance first, then use the big numbers.

88
00:04:07,404 --> 00:04:08,983
Where is this asked?

89
00:04:08,983 --> 00:04:12,725
As mass to mass numericals,
and molarity calculations.

90
00:04:13,325 --> 00:04:14,679
Exam level now.

91
00:04:14,679 --> 00:04:19,946
What mass of oxygen burns eleven
grams of propane, C three H eight, to

92
00:04:19,946 --> 00:04:21,943
carbon dioxide and water?

93
00:04:21,943 --> 00:04:23,354
Pause and solve.

94
00:04:28,754 --> 00:04:30,176
The answer is C.

95
00:04:30,176 --> 00:04:31,246
Forty grams.

96
00:04:31,746 --> 00:04:32,906
Balance first.

97
00:04:32,906 --> 00:04:38,143
C three H eight plus five O two
gives three CO two plus four H two O.

98
00:04:38,643 --> 00:04:42,598
Eleven grams of propane is
zero point two five moles.

99
00:04:43,098 --> 00:04:48,331
Times five, one point two five moles
of oxygen, which is forty grams.

100
00:04:48,331 --> 00:04:51,786
B used sixteen, the
mass of one oxygen atom.

101
00:04:52,386 --> 00:04:53,847
Here is your recap.

102
00:04:53,847 --> 00:04:55,348
Screenshot this card.

103
00:04:58,548 --> 00:05:01,546
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

104
00:05:01,946 --> 00:05:02,846
One.

105
00:05:02,475 --> 00:05:07,394
In the burning of methane, what is
the mole ratio of methane to oxygen?

106
00:05:08,194 --> 00:05:09,094
Two.

107
00:05:08,733 --> 00:05:12,826
Why does one gram of methane
not need two grams of oxygen?

108
00:05:13,626 --> 00:05:14,526
Three.

109
00:05:14,286 --> 00:05:19,253
What is the molarity of two grams
of sodium hydroxide in half a litre?

110
00:05:20,153 --> 00:05:23,338
Next time: what if the
oxygen runs out first?

111
00:05:23,338 --> 00:05:25,580
We find out in limiting reagent.
